Instruction Cache Hit Rates‡GCC instruction cache hit rate. If low, the combined instruction footprint across warps exceeds GCC capacity â€” reduce kernel code size by limiting unrolling, inlining, or splitting large kernels. Very low rates (<90%) can add visible stall cycles to every warp."}
Cpmsampling:gcc__average_cache_request_type_instruction_hit_rate.pctGCC Instruction Hit RateJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"ü
GCC Bandwidth per TypeýGCC bandwidth broken down by request type. Each type has a different peak bandwidth (Instructions: 128B/cyc, Constants: 64B/cyc, Tex/Sampler Descriptors: 32B/cyc).
If Instruction Requests dominate, the kernel has a large code footprint â€” consider reducing it.
If Constant Requests dominate, the constant working set is spilling through GCC â€” compact constant data layouts.
If TSHeader Requests are significant, the kernel uses many unique texture/sampler descriptors â€” try reducing descriptor diversity.␀"x
