&SM Tensor Pipe Instructions per Family¤Identify which tensor instruction families are in use.
If one sub-family dominates, the workload is specialized. If both appear, check whether mixed-precision conversions add overhead. If tensor throughput is low but expected to be high, verify the compiler is generating tensor instructions.␀"˜
`pmsampling:sm__inst_executed_pipe_tensor_subpipe_hmma_realtime.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedTensor HMMA ThroughputJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling"˜
`pmsampling:sm__inst_executed_pipe_tensor_subpipe_imma_realtime.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedTensor IMMA ThroughputJgb20y_TriageCompute_sampling*Ç
SM Compute Pipelines—Identify which SM compute pipe is the throughput limiter. The highest bar is the bottleneck pipe.
If one pipe (FMA, ALU Heavy, Tensor, XU) is clearly dominant, focus optimization on reducing its workload â€” see the instruction-mix and thread-divergence rows below.
If SM Issue Throughput is high but no single pipe dominates, the workload has a balanced instruction mix â€” algorithmic restructuring or library offload may be needed.
If all pipes are low, the SM is not compute-bound â€” return to Top-Level Throughput to re-classify."|
