SM Occupancy & StallsOnly investigate occupancy if SM Issue Throughput (in SM Compute Pipelines) is low. Occupancy is a means, not an end. The goal is hiding latency.
Fewer than 4 warps/SM is a starvation condition: launch bigger grids or increase block count.
Above ~16 warps/SM, additional occupancy rarely helps hide math-pipe latency.
If Warps Inactive (SM Active) is large, an occupancy limiter is preventing more warps from being scheduled - check the resource rows below (registers, shared memory, block count) to find which limit is binding.␀"¼
ATriageCompute.tpc__warps_active.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedWarps ActiveJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingi␀␀␀␀␀␀à?zÁTriageCompute.tpc__warps_active = CounterMetric(RerollUp(smsp__warps_active, RollupOp.sum, HwUnit.tpc), 'cumulative # of warps in flight') TriageCompute.tpc__warps_active:SetSustainedRate(96.0)"ò
LTriageCompute.sm__warps_inactive_sm_active.avg.pct_of_peak_sustained_elapsedWarps Inactive (SM Active)Jgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingzLTriageCompute.max_warps_per_sm = ConversionFactorMetric(48.0, DimUnit.warps)z™TriageCompute.sm__warps_inactive_sm_active = CounterMetric(sm__cycles_active * TriageCompute.max_warps_per_sm - RerollUp(smsp__warps_active, RollupOp.sum, HwUnit.sm), 'cumulative # of idle warp slots on active SMs') TriageCompute.sm__warps_inactive_sm_active:SetSustainedRate(48.0)´
L1 Data Cache & LSU␀"Ï
L1TEX LSU Throughput½Compare the LSUs two stages to classify the bottleneck.
If LSU Data-Stage > LSU Writeback, the bottleneck is in address processing â€” likely poor coalescing (global/local/surface) or bank conflicts (shared). Check per-space breakdown below and Shared Memory Bank Conflicts.
If LSU Writeback > LSU Data-Stage, wide uniform loads (64/128-bit) or high miss traffic is saturating the writeback path â€” check L1TEX Tag-Stage Bandwidth per Hit Miss."|
