DRAM␀"Ò
DRAM BandwidthóDRAM bandwidth split by read and write direction. DRAM is half-duplex â€” reads and writes alternate, and direction changes cost cycles.
If the read/write ratio is roughly balanced, direction-turn overhead may be significant â€” check DRAM Read-to-Write Turns.
If one direction heavily dominates, the DRAM controller can batch efficiently and direction turns are minimal.␀"b
,pmsampling:dram__sectors_read.sum.per_secondDRAM Read BandwidthJgb10x_Compute_Triage_sampling"d
-pmsampling:dram__sectors_write.sum.per_secondDRAM Write BandwidthJgb10x_Compute_Triage_sampling*¬

DRAM Throughput®Compare throughput at L2 Request, L2 Miss, and DRAM stages to understand the amplification from L2 to DRAM. If DRAM Sectors is close to L2 Request Bandwidth, the L2 cache is ineffective â€” most requests reach DRAM.
If DRAM Sectors is near its %-of-peak ceiling, the workload is DRAM-bandwidth limited â€” check L2 Throughput per Source Unit to find the dominant consumer, and DRAM Read-to-Write Turns for protocol-level overhead."é
