CBC Hit Rate¤Lossless compression (CBC) hit rate. A high hit rate means many L2 lines are stored compressed, reducing DRAM bandwidth. A low hit rate means data is not compressible â€” this is normal for most compute data.
If CBC Hit Rate is moderate but DRAM throughput is still the bottleneck, the data has partial compressibility â€” improving write locality and reducing random-pattern writes can increase the compression benefit."“
4TriageCompute.lts__average_cbc_hit_rate_realtime.pctCBC Hit RateJgb20y_TriageCompute_samplingz·TriageCompute.lts__cbc_requests_type_comptag_realtime = CounterMetric(lts__cbc_requests_type_comptag_lookup_hit_realtime + lts__cbc_requests_type_comptag_lookup_miss_or_hit_to_miss_realtime, '# of compressible requests handled by CBC') TriageCompute.lts__cbc_requests_type_comptag_realtime:SetSustainedRate(1.0)zôTriageCompute.lts__average_cbc_hit_rate_realtime = RatioMetric(lts__cbc_requests_type_comptag_lookup_hit_realtime, RollupOp.sum, TriageCompute.lts__cbc_requests_type_comptag_realtime, RollupOp.sum, 1.0, 'proportion of requests that hit in CBC')*Í
L2 Throughput„Find the L2 cache throughput limiter among its internal stages. The L2 serves all VidMem (device memory) aperture accesses between the crossbar and DRAM.
If L2 Sectors is the peak, raw request volume is the limiter â€” check L2 Bandwidth per Source Unit to identify the dominant source.
If XBAR-to-L2 or L2-to-XBAR is the peak, the crossbar interconnect between SMs and L2 is saturated.
If L2 Atomic Stage is high, atomic operations are consuming significant L2 bandwidth â€” consider warp-level reductions to reduce atomic traffic.
If L2 CBC Requests is significant, lossless compression is active â€” check CBC Hit Rate to assess its benefit."p
